# Copy to .env.local and fill in. Committed on purpose — every variable
# documented, no values. The .gitignore keeps .env.local out.

# The diary owner's WebID. On Inrupt ESS this is served by the identity
# provider, not the Pod, and is NOT writable — discover storage from it via
# pim:storage rather than assuming it shares an origin with the Pod.
#
# QUOTE IT. A WebID is a fragment IRI — https://you.example/profile/card#me —
# and Next's env loader treats an unquoted `#` as the start of a comment, so it
# silently stores the value up to the fragment. Measured 2026-09-04 with
# @next/env: `OWNER_WEBID=http://…/card#me` loads as `http://…/card`, the
# document rather than the person. Nothing errors. The studio then reads the
# profile document's own subject instead of the owner's, reports
# "oidcIssuer: expected string, received undefined" with no hint of a fragment,
# and — where the issuer does sit on the document subject — comes up and locks
# the owner out of their own diary, because sameWebId compares fragments and
# `…/card` is not `…/card#me`. Both failures name two WebIDs that look alike.
# So: OWNER_WEBID="https://you.example/profile/card#me", quotes included.
OWNER_WEBID=

# Storage root the diary lives under, with a trailing slash. Also used to build
# next/image remotePatterns; next/image refuses Pod-hosted photos without it.
POD_ROOT=

# Site metadata.
SITE_NAME=
# With or without a trailing slash; lib/config.ts normalises it away. It is
# joined onto paths, including the Solid-OIDC client_id an identity provider
# fetches, where a doubled slash silently drops login into dynamic registration.
SITE_URL=

# Default language tag for text written to the Pod (§6). Not the UI language.
SITE_LANGUAGE=en

# Map tiles. OpenFreeMap needs no key, no account and no cookies — that is why
# it is the default. Point this at a paid provider if you prefer.
MAP_STYLE_URL=https://tiles.openfreemap.org/styles/dark

# There is deliberately no revalidation secret here. The Pod cannot notify the
# app when content changes, so invalidation is an explicit push — POST
# /api/revalidate, and it is unauthenticated on purpose. The studio runs in the
# visitor's browser with no server session, so any value it could send would be
# in the client bundle and readable by everyone; a variable named like a secret
# would only make the next person believe the endpoint is protected. What bounds
# it is the tag allowlist and the rate limiter, both explained (with their
# limits) in app/(public)/api/revalidate/route.ts.
